> **Who sets each Status.** A status nobody owns is a status that lies —
> every value has exactly one setter and one triggering event. Set it when
> the event happens, not at the end of your turn.
>
> | Status | Set by | When |
> | --- | --- | --- |
> | `draft` | planner | it starts writing this file |
> | `spec-approved` | lead | the user approves the spec |
> | `in-progress` | implementer | it starts implementing |
> | `blocked:question` | implementer, or lead | an *Open question* needs the **human** — also set **Blocked since** |
> | `blocked:spec` | implementer | the spec itself is unbuildable; bounced back to the planner (max 1, see *Spec bounces*) |
> | `in-review` | implementer | the diff is written and ready to review |
> | `changes-requested` | lead | it routes a CHANGES_REQUESTED verdict back to the implementer |
> | `testing` | tester | it starts running suites |
> | `done` | lead | step 5 only: ledger closed, review PASS on record, user informed |
>
> The three budget counters above are caps, not suggestions —
> `scripts/verify-state.sh` fails loudly when one is exceeded. Blowing a
> budget is an escalation to the user, never a reason to keep looping.

